hello and welcome to the new world  review a source for everything anime and  manga and this time well i sort of mean  that because after a lot of comments  from you  wonderful people i decided to give a  certain series named jiu-jitsu kaisen  ago and the following will be my initial  impressions basically i've now read the  first  five chapters which i believe is the  first arc of the series named the  introduction arc very creative but just  as with the black clover videos if you  are enjoying this review then please do  like the video and subscribe to the  channel which will tell me rather  conclusively that you  yes you want to see more jiu-jitsu  kaizen content and i should also  probably point out that this is a  strictly manga-based review i haven't  seen the anime yet but it's quite new  and i thought that i'd just stick with  the manga because i'd be sure to run out  of episodes rather rapidly but let's  begin with the premise of the series  yuji  itadori is your average high school  student except he isn't because for  whatever reason he is gifted with  superhuman physical abilities but  instead of using those to  any degree whatsoever he prefers to  spend his time screwing around as part  of the school's occult research club and  during the course of said occult  research the occult research club  accidentally unseals a powerful curse  within a finger belonging to someone  known as the king of curses  shenanigan re-ensues and eventually yuji  eats not just one but  two cursed fingers yum yum and is then  abducted by jujitsu sorcerers and given  a choice of immediate execution or  execution after eating roughly 18 more  fingers or i guess 14 more fingers and  perhaps four  thumbs and so yuji gets pulled out of  high school and gets put into  another high school but a different more  sorcerific high school where he will  live and study until his scheduled  execution and that is the premise of  jujutsu kaisen and yeah i'm pretty down  with it in many ways jiu jitsu kaisen  initially presents itself as a very  standard sort of shona model you know  young high school boy with unique latent  abilities takes up a call to action in  order to help others however i think  that one of the greatest weapons jujutsu  kaisen possesses when combating that  standard framework is the overall  general tone of the series thus far judo  tsukaisen is  unapologetically morbid there were so  many moments in these first five  chapters where i just went  that's uh it's kind of dark but that's  the sort of thing i appreciate because  it indicates to me that the series isn't  going to be afraid to take on deep  subject matter like  say death when you think about it death  really isn't an idea that you'll see  explored all that often in a shonen  series because it can be quite  depressing and reminds even teenagers of  their ultimate fate in life which is to  no longer be living so i was quite  surprised when jiu jitsu kaisen decided  to focus on that  right out of the gate specifically with  the story involving yuji and his  grandfather who passes away over the  course of the chapter but not before  imparting some final words onto yuji  being when it's your time to go make  sure you're surrounded by others  don't end up like me and i don't know  how this plays with a much younger  audience but as someone who at this  point in life has experienced a fairly  substantial amount of family death in a  wide variety of circumstances that line  hit me pretty damn hard  and death goes on to hover like a  specter over just about everything  because what i really enjoy about this  series thus far is it isn't a question  of if yuji will die but when  his fate is sealed but temporarily  suspended i suppose and even yuji is  currently preparing for it by saying  things like  i already know how i'm gonna go out and  i really really  really do hope that the series follows  through with that five chapters is way  too early to tell anything but at this  point i think i might feel just a little  bit cheated if we got to the end of  jujutsu kaisen and yuji did not fulfill  this death prophecy that was set up and  i would much rather see him fulfill his  grandfather's words by dying with lots  of people around him it would be  extremely bittersweet but it's a unique  spin on the genre that i'd very much be  on board with the shonen protagonist not  destined to become the strongest or some  sort of king but instead the protagonist  who is simply  destined to die once again that's pretty  morbid as hell but i'm very interested  in the idea and the world further  reflects this through its main power  system of  curses i don't think i know enough about  them at this stage to draw any real  conclusion but it does seem to have this  nen-like aspect to it where curses are  just invisible to most people not  exposed to very specific circumstances  and only curses can be used to combat  other curses and i actually really love  the metaphor that was used to describe  curses being the use of poison to fend  off other poisons because it presents an  inherent danger with using your power at  all like there is no force of good or  purity in the series it's all bad very  very bad but we need to use the bad to  fight the worse which speaking of  jujitsukai sound is pretty damn well set  up because the goals of our protagonists  just so happen to align pretty much  entirely with the goal of our assumedly  major antagonist yuji wants to find and  eat all of the sukuna pieces in order to  die and then rid the world of his curse  but at the same time sukuna wants yuji  to find all of the pieces to combine and  regain his former power so we have this  really weird but fun dynamic  where the protagonist and antagonists  aren't really competing against each  other in fact they should be very much  helping one another which i think is  pretty insidiously ingenious with that  said if i had to pick a weaker aspect of  the series at this point and remember  it's only five chapters in so it's hard  to judge but i would say the uh the  characters are large beyond ug i can't  really say i connected with many  if any of them and when it comes to yuji  it's mostly through empathy for the  situation with his grandfather everyone  else is kind of difficult to pin down  but yuji seems like a  bit of everything he's got a piece of  that very composed cool guy happening  but he's also got a slice of derpy  eccentricity as well as some super  serious introspective drama he also has  a clear perky quality about him which is  demonstrated with my favorite ugly line  thus far being  my name is yuji itadori i'm into girls  like jennifer lawrence nice to meet you  just tells us everything we need to know  about him it's a perfect introduction i  think it's what we should all be doing  so he has a bit of everything going on  but i feel like we have yet to explore  any of those features in any great  amount of depth which obviously we're so  early on but as a result when i think of  yuji currently i sort of have this  picture of his blank face and no real  strong feelings towards him one way or  another but  i think i like him probably the worst  character in this regard for me would be  fushigoro i have absolutely no feelings  regarding him whatsoever because he's  just sort of  you know there at the moment he's cool  he has a nice design but i don't know a  single thing about him but with that  said for an opening arc that's not at  all necessary because i'm sure he'll  have his time to shine later  in terms of characters though the saving  grace for everything is definitely gojo  when he appeared i was like  oh finally someone in the series with an  amazing design and a clearly defined  personality he's just so expressive that  even though i know nothing about him i  feel like i know  him who he is which is a powerful  jujutsu dude bro with a great sense of  humor that he uses to cope with the  makar profession that he has somehow  found himself in in fact i think my  favorite line of the series so far came  from gojo when we saw the whole  flashback with fushiguro begging gojo to  find a way not to execute yuji and then  we jump cut to the present where gojo  just casually states yuji so let me  reiterate you are going to be executed  that was flawlessly executed black  comedy so gojo is definitely the  character with the greatest sense of  comedy thus far which is nice because it  breaks up the  morbidity happening i suppose everywhere  else and there are definitely other  comical characters like the principal or  our female student nobara but neither of  them are quite as solid as gojo plus  it's incredibly hard not to be intrigued  by someone wearing any form of masking  it makes me wonder what's hiding behind  the blindfold or if he needs to use it  as a condition for his own curse prowess  having briefly brought up nobura she was  a very  late addition to this early arc although  i do quite like her generally  combative style of the hammer and nail  it's quite unrefined  and very very brutal which only goes to  reinforce a lot of what i currently love  about jiu jitsu it's not afraid to dive  into a more grotesque aesthetic which is  especially prominent when it comes to  the cursed bass creatures  and it's also just interesting that the  entire power system of this world  is basically fueled purely by negativity  i believe the quote was pain regret  humiliation the more people dwell on bad  memories the more these places become  receptive to those feelings and it just  makes me wonder if there can ever be  anything resembling a happy ending to a  series like jujutsu kaisen because to  get rid of the world's major threats  you'd need to basically achieve a  utopian society with no negative  emotions whatsoever and that just seems  very  unlikely even for fiction which gives it  a slight flavor of berserk  a story about struggling against the  inevitability of the world itself but  speaking of other manga vibes jujutsu  kaisen hits me  right in the bleach very early bleach  that is when it was a slightly darker  story about a high school student  fighting hollows plaguing the real world  before it turned into  that whatever it turned into and that's  a really good thing because that initial  shade of darkness is what really  attracted me to bleach so i do enjoy  that jujutsu kaisen operates in that  sort of sphere as well  in terms of art something that i have  noticed in these early offerings is that  gege akutami seems to really enjoy  these very narrow panels whether it's  horizontal or vertical akutami makes  very frequent use of these narrow  rectangular shapes rather than opting  for a more rounded square style of  design which is much more common in  manga and i know that was a kind of  stupid thing to say a rounded square and  all but these narrow panels do give the  series a unique aesthetic and page  identity in addition to that akatami  makes very common use of breakout panels  like this one here where the artwork  isn't actually contained within the  shape of the panel another mangaka do  this on occasion but it's usually  only for big dramatic events like  character reveals or some sort of action  piece otherwise the large majority of  shonen authors very much play within the  boundaries of their established panels  but i really love akatami style here  because it gives you the illusion of a  satisfying and rigid panel structure but  it allows the images within those panels  themselves to truly stand out even when  we're just engaged in basic dialogue so  it's very much like the panels are  acting as a background frame to the  image rather than containing the image  if that makes sense and i know that's  probably a really tiny thing that i  didn't need anywhere near that long to  praise but it's a piece of unique  aesthetic flair yet  another piece of unique aesthetic flair  that i like about the series and one  more thing i do enjoy about the art  specifically is that akitami makes very  deliberate decisions regarding detail  for example when we're in a more casual  day-to-day situation we are given this  very flat character rod and often with  no background whatsoever just often pure  white or black negative space meanwhile  as soon as something curse related comes  up this unimaginable amount of detail  just springs into existence and it's  almost as if it's breaking the war  between manga and reality which once  again is very subtle decision but it's  quite a cool device to attention to any  given scene now as for how this arc ends  all i can say is that this is pretty  damn ballsy we basically arrive at the  end of the introduction only to be met  with a mission report from the future  which basically states that one of our  first-year students is going to die so  yuji  nobura or fushiguro once again bringing  the motif of death right back into  things it's the sort of thing that made  me really want to launch into the next  stock but the more i think about it the  more kind of skeptical i become the  boldest possible decision would probably  be to either kill nubra or fush guru but  at the same time i have to ask myself  why because they've both just been  introduced and have yet to be exported  all but then again if it's yuji then  it's kind of false drama because he'd  have to be revived somehow  or die purely on a technicality to  continue the series because as much as  he is resigned to death we have  some more digits to consume before that  can happen so i really did like the  ballsy ending i just have many doubts  that said balls will be ultimately  delivered with that said i think  jujitsukai sen is off to an incredibly  strong start i'm not wildly attached to  the characters but the whole concept and  tone has me incredibly gripped there's  only one other thing i forgot to mention  actually which is one of the principal's  lines that stated  this i can say for sure no jiu-jitsu  sorcerer dies without regret which is  just another in a long line of rather  grisly statements and ideas  all presented to us within this initial  set of chapters and i have to say i  am all on board for it but that pretty  much does it for this blind review of  the introduction arc of jiu jitsu kaisen  and what do you guys think please do  leave your thoughts in the comments  below or even join my discord server and  if you are keen to see more jujutsu  sorcery then please do check out some of  my other videos or even subscribe to the  channel for very maybe regular  jujutsukai send morbidity delivered  straight into your youtube  feed but for now this has been the new  world review and i'll see you next time

